      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the filling: fry the shallots in oil until softened. Add the pork belly and cook for 3 minutes. Add the prawns and cook for 2 more minutes until pink. Season with fish sauce and sugar. Cool completely."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the dough: put the tapioca starch in a bowl. Pour in the boiling water all at once and stir quickly with a wooden spoon. The dough will be hot. Keep working it until it comes together into a smooth, pliable ball."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the warm dough into about 20 pieces. Working quickly, flatten each piece into a thin round disc about 8 cm across."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place a teaspoon of filling in the centre of each disc. Fold the dough over the filling to make a half-moon shape. Press the edges firmly to seal."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Line a steamer basket with banana leaf or baking paper. Arrange the dumplings in a single layer without touching."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Steam over boiling water for 8 to 10 minutes until the dumplings are completely translucent."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the dipping sauce and serve the hot dumplings immediately with the sauce and crispy fried shallots if you have them."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The dough cracks and breaks when folding.",
              "cause": "The dough cooled down too much before shaping.",
              "fix": "Work with the dough while it is still warm. Keep it covered with a damp cloth. If it cools and cracks, knead in a few drops of warm water."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The dumplings stay white and opaque after steaming.",
              "cause": "They were not steamed for long enough.",
              "fix": "Steam for at least 8 minutes. The dumplings should turn completely clear when done. A still-white patch means undercooked dough."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The dumplings stick to the steamer and tear.",
              "cause": "The steamer was not lined or was not oiled.",
              "fix": "Line the steamer with banana leaf, cabbage leaves, or lightly oiled baking paper. Never place dumplings directly on a metal steamer rack."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bánh bột lọc comes from Huế in central Vietnam, the seat of the Nguyễn dynasty. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Huế is famous for its complex, refined snack foods that developed in the royal court. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The dish is sold at street food stalls throughout the city, often wrapped in banana leaf. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Central Vietnamese people eat these dumplings as a snack rather than a main meal. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Visitors to Huế are encouraged to eat them as one of the most distinctive local dishes."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
